  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17336v1</id>
    <title>Back to Pixels: Lifting Latent Video Diffusion into Pixel Space</title>
    <updated>2026-11-12T18:59:55Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17336v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17336v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Frontier video generation models work in a VAE latent space that compresses video in both space and time. This compression made training tractable, but now limits high-resolution, controllable, and interactive generation -- doubling resolution quadruples token count, conditions apply to groups of frames rather than individual ones, and each response must wait for the rest of its multi-frame block. We present PixVid, a simple yet effective method that lifts a pretrained latent video model to directly generate every frame in pixel space. PixVid keeps the diffusion transformer and replaces the VAE decoder with a convolutional head that renders its outputs into pixels. We find that predicting clean video rather than velocity, together with a larger noise shift, is key to quality. Finetuned from Wan2.1-14B, PixVid is competitive with the latent baseline within $\sim 1.3$M training clips. Working framewise in pixels, it generates at $2\times$ higher resolution with the same per-frame token count, keeps depth-conditioned video editing aligned under fast motion, and responds to each action at the next frame. Project page: https://chenshuo20.github.io/PixVid/</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-12T18:59:55Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Project Page: https://chenshuo20.github.io/PixVid/</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
