  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16409v1</id>
    <title>Experience-Handoff Reinforcement Learning for Recursive Self-Improvement</title>
    <updated>2026-11-12T07:18:30Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16409v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16409v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>It is common to train Large Language Models (LLMs) for agentic tasks with Reinforcement Learning (RL) from scalar rewards. However, this throws away the fine-grained information about the environment that the agent experienced during the trial (e.g., error logs). To utilize this rich information, recent works wrap the agent in a harness that refines its own inputs through reflection or compaction and hands past experience to future rollouts, but the construction of the handed-off artifacts (e.g., a playbook) is itself not learned. We want the best of both worlds: gradient updates from a scalar reward, while also utilizing environment information to hand off experience. As a concrete testbed, we fix the Agentic Context Engineering (\ace{}) harness and train one model to act, reflect, and curate task-local playbooks. Our method, \acerl{}, evaluates candidate experience-handoff artifacts by their downstream effect on future actor rollouts, and co-optimizes the role-specific behaviors (action generator, insight reflector, and playbook curator) under a fixed scaffold. Across agentic benchmarks, \acerl{} achieves higher held-out performance than baseline methods. The results suggest that agent training should optimize not only the actions in the current rollout, but also the model-written experience that future rollouts inherit.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-12T07:18:30Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Doyoung Kim</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Byeongguk Jeon</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kailin Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Sherry Yang</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16321v1</id>
    <title>From Large Language Models to Efficient System One Models via Adaptive Computation</title>
    <updated>2026-11-12T05:40:37Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16321v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16321v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Transferring LLMs into System One decision models typically replaces the language modeling head with a decision head while retaining the full backbone, making every decision pay for all layers. Yet it remains unclear how much depth such decisions require. We find that decision information is concentrated in intermediate layers, with early layers building essential features and the final six layers removable without loss, while independently trained smaller models largely mimic the full model rather than developing specialized decision capabilities. These findings suggest that efficiency requires not simply smaller backbones, but shared computation across different depths. Building on this, we propose \method{}, which jointly trains a full model with prefix sub-models that share its final layers and decision head through self-distillation. A lightweight gate reads the hidden state at each exit to determine whether additional computation is needed, allowing one model to operate at multiple layers. Across seven decision benchmarks, our approach matches or outperforms independently trained models at every depth, while achieving better accuracy-efficiency trade-offs through adaptive computation.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-12T05:40:37Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Minh-Anh Nguyen</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16517v1</id>
    <title>UniSpark: Exploring Hybrid Attention for Multimodal Understanding and Generation</title>
    <updated>2026-11-12T09:16:10Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16517v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16517v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>We present UniSpark-9B, a unified multimodal model that investigates hybrid attention for visual understanding and generation. Built on a Mixture-of-Transformer-Experts backbone with approximately 9B parameters in each branch, UniSpark combines full attention for explicit token interactions with GatedDeltaNet linear attention for compact context propagation. Autoregressive text prediction and visual flow matching jointly support visual question answering, text-to-image generation, instruction-guided editing, and interleaved text-image prediction. In interleaved generation, noisy visual targets are isolated from subsequent predictions, while clean visual representations provide context for later outputs. A blockwise protocol enforces this distinction through attention masks in full-attention layers and recurrent-state retention rules in linear-attention layers. The selected training sources contain 159.7M generation samples, 213.4M understanding samples, and 624.8K interleaved sequences. A four-stage curriculum jointly trains multimodal understanding and visual generation, refining answer accuracy and instruction following while extending image synthesis to 2K. UniSpark scores 2470.3 on MME-S and 0.83 on GenEval. Qualitative examples further illustrate its capabilities in interleaved image generation, video synthesis, and video editing. Together, these results demonstrate that hybrid attention provides an effective backbone for unified multimodal understanding and generation.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-12T09:16:10Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Technical Report, 31 pages</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
