  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09567v1</id>
    <title>TurtLES: A Large-Scale Benchmark for Turbulent 3D Neural PDE Surrogates</title>
    <updated>2026-11-06T15:07:53Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09567v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.09567v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Machine-learning surrogates are increasingly used to accelerate computational fluid dynamics, yet progress is limited by the lack of benchmarks capturing realistic, time-dependent turbulent flows. We introduce TurtLES, a 14 TB dataset of 3D turbulent cylinder wakes in the shear-layer transition regime, computed via high-fidelity implicit large-eddy simulations. The dataset contains 400 trajectories of 400 time steps each, with 3-11 million points per frame, spanning variations in geometry, Reynolds number, and angle of attack. Unlike existing datasets, TurtLES combines (i) physically realistic turbulence with an active energy cascade driven by vortex stretching, (ii) dense irregular meshes, and (iii) long-horizon temporal dynamics. We benchmark state-of-the-art neural operators on autoregressive rollouts and identify key failure modes, including temporal instability, loss of small-scale energy, and challenges in learning on large irregular domains. TurtLES provides a new benchmark for evaluating surrogate models on 3D turbulent wakes at industrially relevant mesh resolution.</summary>
    <category term="physics.flu-dyn" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-06T15:07:53Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="physics.flu-dyn"/>
    <author>
      <name>Jarno Platenburg</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Armand Kassaï Koupaï</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Paola Cinnella</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Patrick Gallinari</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10439v1</id>
    <title>Who Remembers What? Multi-Granularity Memory Extraction for Multi-User, Multi-Agent Interaction</title>
    <updated>2026-11-07T03:35:51Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10439v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10439v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Self-evolving agents improve their own skills, memory, and harness code from experience without retraining the base LLM, and group-evolving agents go further by sharing these lessons across agents. Yet most such systems overlook that sharing across users and heterogeneous agents requires deciding who can benefit from each lesson and what information must remain private. We present MixUA, a memory system that distills independent user-agent trajectories into lessons and assigns each lesson to a user-agent pair, user, agent, or global scope according to whom it benefits and what it may disclose. For evaluation, we introduce CrossLearnBench, a mixed-user-agent benchmark that pairs established agent tasks with user preferences and privacy probes that test whether private facts leak into memory other users can read. With five users and seven agents, MixUA raises pass rates by up to 16 points over no memory and 5 over a single global memory, even on tasks previously tried only by other users on other agents. It retains up to 90% of previously stated preferences, and at most 0.02% of privacy probes find a planted private fact in a forbidden scope. We hope MixUA offers a scalable path toward a global system where users and agents co-evolve.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.MA" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-07T03:35:51Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yunzhe Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Mesut Erhan Unal</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Sheng Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Shabnam Ghadar</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Peng Tang</name>
    </author>
