  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17190v1</id>
    <title>Consensus Capture in Reference-Based UAV Video Stabilization: A Benchmark on Dynamic Urban Scenes</title>
    <updated>2026-11-12T17:55:06Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17190v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17190v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Reference-frame stabilization registers every frame of a hovering-drone video onto one reference frame, keeping object positions comparable. The registration pipelines it relies on are selected on feature-matching benchmarks in which only the camera moves. Over a busy intersection, vehicles moving together supply mutually consistent correspondences that can draw the robust homography estimate toward their motion: consensus capture, which inlier statistics cannot flag. A two-part benchmark measures it and the object masking meant to prevent it. A static protocol scores ten pipelines under exactly known homographies on 4K aerial frames, tuned on a site-disjoint split at a matched resolution and feature budget. A dynamic protocol scores them at an offline and a real-time budget on hovers selected from the video alone to span the amount and coherence of moving foreground, plus a stationary control. It measures background alignment, object position error and the share of a moving group's displacement an estimate follows, against a pseudo-ground-truth reference audited with manually annotated ground control points, which also score every candidate. On static scenes, tuned AKAZE, RootSIFT, SIFT and BRISK lead, retuning the matcher thresholds raises the mean average accuracy (mAA) by at most 0.0062, and masking costs less than 0.005. Unmasked ORB is captured on 28 of 304 real-time dynamic pairs and on 6 offline; its real-time captured estimates have a median corner error of 18.8 px yet typical inlier counts and ratios. Masking removes capture for every pipeline and leaves the leading pipelines unchanged. Code, data and results are released.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-12T17:55:06Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>50 pages, 17 figures, 24 tables. Code at https://github.com/rfonod/stabilo-benchmark and data at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23623540 and https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.15050577</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Robert Fonod</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Nikolas Geroliminis</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16961v1</id>
    <title>The Value of Thinking: Learning When to Reason in Hybrid Decision Models</title>
    <updated>2026-11-12T15:32:47Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16961v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16961v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Existing adaptive-reasoning methods shorten the explicit reasoning a model writes, or skip it by switching modes; either way the answer is still written out token by token. \emph{Rather than deciding how much to think}, we explore a different paradigm, \emph{zero-or-$T$ thinking}: a single model either answers from the implicit thinking inside one forward pass, generating no token, or performs full autoregressive reasoning at a cost of $T$ tokens when that is worth paying. We realise it in a \emph{hybrid decision model} (HDM), where one frozen backbone serves both modes and a lightweight \emph{self-routing block} chooses between them. The block estimates the \emph{value of thinking}, the utility the slow mode is expected to add over the fast one, from what that implicit thinking has already produced, and the slow mode runs only where that value exceeds a price $λ$ set per request. Where no per-decision label exists, as in episodic agentic tasks, we introduce \emph{Calibrated Decision Policy Optimisation} (CDPO), a reinforcement-learning recipe that trains the block from episode-level returns. We evaluate HDM on MMLU-Pro, AGIEval, GPQA-Diamond, JevBench, BANKING77 and MiniWoB. Specifically, on JevBench HDM-4B scores $0.857$ against Jev's $0.851$ ($+0.7\%$) at a median $0.179$\,s against $0.652$ ($-72.5\%$). On the agentic venue MiniWoB HDM-27B cuts token cost by $80.1\%$.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-12T15:32:47Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>20 pages, 3 figures</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Zhengxu Yu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ke Xu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Meng Fang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Haitham Ammar</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Weilin Luo</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jun Wang</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.15844v1</id>
    <title>Open Vision Banana: A Fully Open Recipe for Generative Visual Perception</title>
    <updated>2026-11-11T18:57:34Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.15844v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.15844v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Recent work suggests that pretrained image generators encode real-world understanding beyond image synthesis. Vision Banana exploits this by framing vision tasks as conditional image editing. However, its data, weights, code, and implementation details are not public. We present Open Vision Banana (OVB), a fully open and reproducible recipe for converting pretrained generators into unified multi-task perception systems. Our analyses identify the design choices that matter most for generalization, including color encoding, prompt design, parameter adaptation, and task balancing. These choices interact, so individually helpful choices can hurt when combined with others. Starting from 4B and 9B parameter open-weight base models and using 2.1M-3.8M training pairs from public datasets, OVB surpasses Vision Banana's reported Cityscapes zero-shot performance (73.9 vs. 69.9 mIoU) and rivals state-of-the-art affine-invariant depth estimators. Its non-zero-shot variants come within five points of Vision Banana on referring and reasoning segmentation but remain further behind on instance segmentation. All models largely retain the base model's image-editing abilities. With legend ensembling, we introduce a test-time scaling method for generator-based perception. It segments an image under several class-color legends and fuses the results by majority vote, correcting unstable color-class bindings and improving mIoU by up to 8.7 points on Cityscapes and 15.0 points on COCO-Object without retraining. We release the dataset construction and processing pipeline, the training and evaluation code, as well as the final, intermediate, and ablation checkpoints. Project website: https://openvisionbanana.github.io</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-11T18:57:34Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Arpit Jadon</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Valentinos Pariza</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jona Ruthardt</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ryousuke Yamada</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>A. Sophia Koepke</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Andrei Bursuc</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yuki M. Asano</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00428v1</id>
    <title>Do Multimodal Models Decide Consistently on Equivalent Optical Spectra?</title>
    <updated>2026-10-30T22:21:38Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00428v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.00428v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>A spectrum can be given to a model as a table in nanometers or as a plot in photon energy, and the decision should not depend on which one it sees. When the data needed for an answer are missing, the model should say so. We test representation invariance and abstention on measured optical spectra of silicon metasurfaces (MOCLIP) and gold nanoparticles, with labels computed from the data. Each spectrum is shown as four tables (nm, $μ$m, scientific notation, eV) and two plots (nm, eV), and partial spectra remove data inside or outside the queried range. We evaluate the decision models d3-nano and Jev 1.13, three GPT-6 Luna configurations and the Qwen3.5-2B base model. Changing only the representation moves accuracy by up to 41 percentage points within one model, mostly on photon-energy tables and plots. With the same evidence, asking in photon energy instead of wavelength helps some model-view pairs by 16-50 points and hurts others by up to 25. When the relevant extremum is removed, unadapted models rarely answer "undetermined". LoRA fine-tuning of d3-nano improves comparisons on the trained table formats, but much of its gain on unanswerable questions comes from a shortcut: it abstains whenever the input is truncated. Training with answerable truncations avoids the shortcut in all six seeds, with a 90-100 point gap between abstention after inside and outside cuts on held-out structures; with the same amount of extra original-task data, five of six seeds keep the shortcut. On gold spectra the two training sets perform similarly. Multimodal language models used for scientific decisions should be tested on equivalent representations and on missing evidence, not only on average accuracy. We release the tasks, per-response results and analysis code.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="physics.optics" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-30T22:21:38Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>20 pages, 4 figures, 11 tables. Code and data: https://github.com/zwang-devv/spectra-decision-audit</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Ziyuan Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Genliang Zhu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xiaobo Chen</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08538v1</id>
    <title>Subliminal Learning in Vision-Language Models</title>
    <updated>2026-11-04T23:36:01Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08538v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.08538v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Subliminal learning is a phenomenon in large language models (LLMs) where a teacher passes a hidden preference to a student through training data unrelated to that preference. We study whether subliminal learning also occurs in vision-language models (VLMs). We finetune a teacher VLM to prefer a specific animal or facial attribute and use a control teacher from the same base model. Both teachers generate numbers for an unrelated visual task involving geometric shapes. Two students initialized from the same base VLM are finetuned on the same images, using numbers generated by either the biased or control teacher. Across VLMs ranging from 2B to 7B parameters, we find that both animal and facial preferences can transfer through this unrelated task. Students trained on biased-teacher outputs select the target more often than control students, with a difference of up to $\sim$10 percentage points in the animal preference task. Transfer can become stronger with more training data and varies with the target preference, model, and how the numbers are generated. Beyond preferences, we show that the biased student's explanations use more of the same words and phrases as the biased teacher's explanations than those of the control student, even when all three models select the same image. These results matter for training VLMs and show that training on unrelated data may change both a VLM's image preferences and how it explains its choices, and that transfer can become stronger with more training samples.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="eess.IV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-04T23:36:01Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Accepted to WACV 2027</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
    <author>
      <name>Arman Hatami</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Romina Aalishah</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ilya E. Monosov</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12872v1</id>
    <title>HiVe-Mem: Interleaving Horizontal Memory Expansion and Vertical Evidence Composition for Multimodal Agents</title>
    <updated>2026-11-09T18:57:00Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12872v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12872v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>As multimodal agents accumulate increasingly long interaction histories, effectively retrieving and utilizing past information becomes critical for long-term interaction. Existing memory systems typically rely on semantic retrieval over compressed memories and expose predefined representations, which may miss relevant memories and overlook heterogeneous evidence needs across queries. We introduce \textbf{\hivemem}, a multimodal memory framework that interleaves \textbf{\textsc{H}}or\textbf{\textsc{i}}zontal Memory Expansion with \textbf{\textsc{Ve}}rtical Evidence Composition. For horizontal memory expansion, \hivemem organizes memory episodes into a relational graph using textual and visual anchors, expanding semantically retrieved seeds with related neighbors to recover complementary information. For vertical evidence composition, each retrieved memory retains multiple evidence forms, including compact summaries, captions, grounded visual views, raw images, and interaction history. A lightweight trainable selector independently composes an evidence subset for each retrieved memory, enabling query-adaptive utilization of heterogeneous multimodal information. This two-dimensional design jointly broadens memory coverage and adapts the evidence contributed by individual memories. Experiments across three multimodal memory benchmarks show that \hivemem consistently improves task performance across base models while reducing token consumption by about 90\% compared with the most efficient competing multimodal baselines.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-09T18:57:00Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Code is available at https://github.com/ViktorAxelsen/HiVe-Mem</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Haozhen Zhang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Bohan Liu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jianzhu Bao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Qingyuan Liu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Haodong Yue</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xiao Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zi Pong Lim</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Wenya Wang</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2607.13920v2</id>
    <title>DeepStress: Stress-Testing Deep Search Agents</title>
    <updated>2026-11-12T13:18:48Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.13920v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.13920v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>While search agents demonstrate impressive capabilities in multi-step question answering, their robustness to poor-quality evidence remains under-explored. Such evidence is rare in benchmark corpora, and resulting failures are hard to reproduce. We propose DeepStress, a dynamic stress-testing framework that subjects agents to degraded evidence at controlled rates. It replaces the retrieval module with a synthetic environment where documents are generated dynamically for each query, under independent control of their trustworthiness, relevance, and factuality. To characterize behavior under stress, we go beyond binary accuracy with a five-way classification of final-answer outcomes and two new reliability-aware metrics, RAS and CoPRAS, which credit cautious behavior and account for computational cost. Evaluating 12 systems on questions from HotpotQA and BrowseComp-Plus, we find that several open-weight agents answer confidently when sources contain obvious credibility red flags, and that many HotpotQA questions are answered from parametric knowledge even when all returned documents are factually wrong.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-07-15T14:59:29Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>8 pages preprint</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Ismael Rousseau</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Geraldine Damnati</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Frederic Bechet</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16728v1</id>
    <title>EgoX: Egocentric Cross-Embodiment Manipulation with Embodiment Dreaming</title>
    <updated>2026-11-12T12:40:24Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16728v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16728v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Generalist manipulation policies can benefit from the complementary experience of humans, real robots, and simulated agents. However, differences in morphology, sensing, and control interfaces make it difficult to learn effectively from their combined demonstrations. We introduce EgoX, an egocentric cross-embodiment learning framework that organizes heterogeneous demonstrations through semantically corresponding observation and action modalities. EgoX pretrains a Modularized Cross-Embodiment Transformer with Embodiment Dreaming (MXT+) on this mixture and fine-tunes it on target-embodiment demonstrations. Within each embodiment, MXT+ assigns a dedicated tokenizer to each observation modality and a dedicated expert to each action modality, with a Transformer trunk shared across embodiments. A training-only embodiment-dreaming objective predicts latent representations of future visual and proprioceptive states, encouraging the shared trunk to model each embodiment's dynamics. Across 13 task-embodiment pairs spanning five tasks and six robot embodiments, including five physical robots and one simulated humanoid, cross-embodiment pretraining raises MXT+'s average normalized task progress from 0.63 to 0.80 over training from scratch. The gains are particularly pronounced under out-of-distribution conditions, where average task progress increases from 0.53 to 0.77. Ablations demonstrate the benefits of broader pretraining mixtures, modular interfaces, and embodiment dreaming, with dreaming producing more similar latent geometry across embodiments. Project page: egox-dream.github.io.</summary>
    <category term="cs.RO" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-12T12:40:24Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.RO"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yaru Niu</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Binghong Chen</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Changwei Yao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Shangtao Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Zhenlong Fang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Revanth Krishna Senthilkumaran</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Shuai Zhou</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Yuemin Mao</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Kalie Ching</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Po-Ju Chao</name>
