  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.15777v1</id>
    <title>SO-Arena: Benchmarking the Capability Spectrum and Reliability of System One Models</title>
    <updated>2026-11-11T17:53:45Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.15777v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.15777v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>System One models promise fast, general-purpose decision-making without lengthy token generation. However, understanding their capability spectrum and decision reliability requires more than aggregate performance on current benchmarks. Existing evaluations often provide limited insight into the breadth of model capabilities and the reliability of their decisions. We introduce SO-Arena, a benchmark characterizing the capability spectrum and decision reliability of System One models across 11 domains and five capability families. For the capability spectrum, SO-Arena offers evidence-grounded evaluation through reliable sources and manual verification of reference labels against model-visible inputs; fine-grained capability assessment through domain-specific difficulty criteria; and realistic interactive evaluation, where models iteratively execute actions in sandboxes. Reasoning-intensive tasks further enable system one-two comparison. Beyond accuracy, SO-Arena assesses decision reliability through two complementary measures: decision stability under controlled option-set changes and missing-answer recognition when no valid option is available. Evaluations across 21 models reveal that the benefits of scaling and additional reasoning vary substantially across domains. More importantly, strong overall performance does not necessarily imply reliable decision-making: the best-performing System One model ranks only seventh in reliability. Together, these findings highlight the need to characterize capability spectrum and decision reliability as complementary dimensions, providing a systematic view of the strengths and limitations of System One models.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-11T17:53:45Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>32 pages, 10 figures, 7 tables. Project page: https://lichenlillc.github.io/SO-Arena</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
      <name>Lichen Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Ziping Dong</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Sicheng Feng</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Qi Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xinchao Wang</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17334v1</id>
    <title>Unbundling Multimodal Vision Encoding</title>
    <updated>2026-11-12T18:59:54Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17334v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17334v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>How multimodal language models should encode visual input remains up for debate. Most models use a pretrained vision encoder, while some recent recipes train the encoder from scratch with the language model or remove it entirely. Vision encoders bundle together three design choices: a visual prior from upstream pretraining, dedicated vision capacity, and pre-fusion placement of that capacity (i.e., before the multimodal backbone). Because published models also differ in data, architecture, and compute, comparing them cannot isolate the effect of each choice. We disentangle these choices via a controlled framework where we pretrain all models from scratch on common data and compare them on visual question answering and language benchmarks at matched lifetime compute. We find that the visual prior is not necessary; an encoder trained jointly from scratch is on par with a pretrained one, without a separate training stage. What matters instead is dedicated vision capacity: an encoder-free model trails on visual understanding even when its backbone is enlarged by the encoder's parameter count. Yet that capacity need not precede multimodal fusion, as vision-only modules added in-fusion to early backbone layers perform comparably to a pre-fusion encoder and can lower time to first token. These results also hold across backbone sizes, when encoder pretraining is treated as free, and when starting from a pretrained language model. Overall, our findings favor dedicated vision capacity trained jointly with the backbone and placed early within it.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CV" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-11-12T18:59:54Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Project page: https://unbundling.github.io</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CV"/>
